>>>

<<<
felloe, felly. 'In England the

forms seem to be equally in good

use °' (OED, which pronounces each

as it is spelt). Perhaps
===
**felloe, felly.** 'In England the forms seem to be equally in good use' (OED, which pronounces each as it is spelt). Perhaps
>>>

<<<
the prevailing usage is to spell -oe, & say fé'li.
===
the prevailing usage is to spell *-oe*, & say fĕ'lĭ.
>>>

<<<
fellow & hyphens. See HYPHENS for
===
**fellow** & hyphens. See ^^HYPHENS^^ for
>>>

<<<
e.g. fellow man, fellow-man, & fellowman. Usage,
===
e.g. *fellow man*, *fellow-man*, & *fellowman*. Usage,
>>>

<<<
far from observing those principles with f.; they
===
far from observing those principles with *f.*; they
>>>

<<<
would require that f. man, if man is to bear the accent, as it does, should be written either f. man or fellowman, & that if f.-man is written
===
would require that *f. man*, if *man* is to bear the accent, as it does, should be written either *f. man* or *fellowman*, & that if *f.-man* is written
>>>

<<<
accented on fellow only; but f.-man is almost
===
accented on *fellow* only; but *f.-man* is almost
>>>

<<<
All the combinations of f. with a noun (except. f.-feeling, for which see below) would
===
All the combinations of *f.* with a noun (except. *f.-feeling*, for which see below) would
>>>

<<<
F.-feeling, which is more of a true compound than the rest (f. not being another name for feeling, as it is for soldier in f. soldier), would be better written fellowfeeling, but
===
*F.-feeling*, which is more of a true compound than the rest (*f.* not being another name for *feeling*, as it is for *soldier* in *f. soldier*), would be better written *fellowfeeling*, but
>>>

<<<
parallel to the f. phrases; sensible
===
parallel to the *f.* phrases; sensible
>>>

